{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778979515792,"sessionID":"ses_1cc8f4f7fffeGdNCtRJTOfYiA5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_HIdmqv1YjVvfhEmP4GZmkJxC","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"jq -r '.body' \"__issue_context__.json\"","timeout":120000,"workdir":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl","description":"Prints full issue body text"},"output":"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\n\n\n**Preferred term label**\n- CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD4 T cell\n- CD4+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD4-positive T cell\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD8 T cell\n- CD8+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD8-positive T cell\n\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation(e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including the transcription factor TOX, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and T-bet, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and tissue environment.\n(PMID:31390978; PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumours, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including expression of the transcription factor TOX and reduced T-bet, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and EOMES, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and disease context.\n(PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000624)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000625)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\n**Required markers**\n- expresses some 'PD-1 (PDCD1)'\n\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\n\n\nNotes on placement:\nThe existing exhausted T cell (CL_0011025) is a lineage-free abstract class and should not be used as the parent.\nFollowing the existing structure of CL, lineage-defined subsets (memory, helper, naive) are placed under CD4-positive or CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell. Therefore, these exhausted subsets should also be placed directly under CD4-positive and CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell.\n\nKey references:\n- PMID:31390978 — Human CD4 exhausted T cells with sustained PD-1 expression.\n- PMID:31207603 — CD8 exhausted T cells showing persistent PD-1 and TOX-dependent programming.\n- PMID:36907685 — Exhausted T cell differentiation stages; PD-1 as the most consistent marker.\n- PMID:38166256 — Latest review summarizing exhaustion heterogeneity and PD-1 as universal hallmark.\n\n**Your ORCID**\n0000-0001-5742-4697\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\n\n**Additional notes or concerns**\n\n\n\n","metadata":{"output":"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\n\n\n**Preferred term label**\n- CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD4 T cell\n- CD4+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD4-positive T cell\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD8 T cell\n- CD8+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD8-positive T cell\n\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation(e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including the transcription factor TOX, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and T-bet, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and tissue environment.\n(PMID:31390978; PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumours, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including expression of the transcription factor TOX and reduced T-bet, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and EOMES, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and disease context.\n(PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000624)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000625)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\n**Required markers**\n- expresses some 'PD-1 (PDCD1)'\n\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\n\n\nNotes on placement:\nThe existing exhausted T cell (CL_0011025) is a lineage-free abstract class and should not be used as the parent.\nFollowing the existing structure of CL, lineage-defined subsets (memory, helper, naive) are placed under CD4-positive or CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell. Therefore, these exhausted subsets should also be placed directly under CD4-positive and CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell.\n\nKey references:\n- PMID:31390978 — Human CD4 exhausted T cells with sustained PD-1 expression.\n- PMID:31207603 — CD8 exhausted T cells showing persistent PD-1 and TOX-dependent programming.\n- PMID:36907685 — Exhausted T cell differentiation stages; PD-1 as the most consistent marker.\n- PMID:38166256 — Latest review summarizing exhaustion heterogeneity and PD-1 as universal hallmark.\n\n**Your ORCID**\n0000-0001-5742-4697\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\n\n**Additional notes or concerns**\n\n\n\n","exit":0,"description":"Prints full issue body text","truncated":false},"title":"Prints full issue body text","time":{"start":1778979515762,"end":1778979515769}},"id":"prt_e33712d48001WrFSJrNxTA6Gcw","sessionID":"ses_1cc8f4f7fffeGdNCtRJTOfYiA5","messageID":"msg_e33710d85001nY7Skxy5G3TDV9"}}
